WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.980
How does the Myhill Nerode

2
00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.540
theorem measure the memory a

3
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.960
deterministic automaton needs? Two prefixes

4
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.080
require different memories when some

5
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.600
common continuation makes exactly one

6
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.160
resulting word belong to the

7
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.800
language. We will use that

8
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.140
idea both for impossibility and

9
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.600
for an exact state lower

10
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:18.900
bound.

11
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.200
If a deterministic machine reaches

12
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.680
the same state after x

13
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.300
and y, it must react

14
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.460
identically to every later suffix.

15
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.300
Thus one distinguishing suffix proves

16
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.220
those prefixes cannot share a

17
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.060
state. A set of pairwise

18
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.340
distinguishable prefixes gives a lower

19
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.360
bound on the number of

20
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:36.640
states.

21
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.600
For equal zeros followed by

22
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.200
equal ones, consider every prefix

23
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.540
consisting only of zeros. Take

24
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.240
two different lengths n and

25
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.040
m, and append n ones

26
00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.980
to both. The first result

27
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.840
has matching counts, while the

28
00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.760
second does not, so this

29
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.140
single suffix distinguishes that arbitrary

30
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:55.580
pair.

31
00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.640
There are infinitely many prefixes

32
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.060
in that pairwise distinguishable set.

33
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.780
No finite-state machine can assign

34
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.260
all of them different states,

35
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.800
so the language is nonregular.

36
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.180
Unlike a failed pumping attempt,

37
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.360
this directly establishes an unbounded

38
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.280
memory requirement.

39
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.220
Formally, indistinguishability means every appended

40
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.080
suffix gives the same membership

41
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.000
verdict. This is an equivalence

42
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.040
relation, partitioning all words into

43
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.500
classes. The theorem says regularity

44
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.200
is equivalent to finitely many

45
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.780
classes, and the number of

46
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.900
classes equals the smallest possible

47
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.200
deterministic-state count.

48
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.600
For an exact finite example,

49
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.840
take the language containing only

50
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.440
a over the unary alphabet.

51
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.340
A machine needs a start

52
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.000
state, a final state after

53
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.420
one a, and a sink

54
00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.620
after too many symbols. This

55
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.200
gives a three-state upper bound,

56
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:48.960
but drawing three states alone

57
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:49.960
does not prove they are

58
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:50.440
necessary.

59
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.960
Use epsilon, a, and a

60
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:54.720
a as the three distinguishing

61
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.540
prefixes. The suffix epsilon separates

62
00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.200
a from each other prefix,

63
00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.600
because only a is already

64
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.400
in the language. The suffix

65
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.720
a separates epsilon from a

66
00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.640
a, producing a versus three

67
00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:08.580
copies of a.

68
00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.080
Every pair in the three-element

69
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.560
set is distinguished, so every

70
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.500
deterministic machine for this language

71
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.160
needs at least three states.

72
00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.520
Together with the three-state construction,

73
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.260
that proves the machine minimal.

74
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.380
A lower bound and matching

75
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.120
upper bound are both needed

76
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.280
for an exact answer.

77
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.220
The suffix may depend on

78
00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.000
the chosen pair; there is

79
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.080
no requirement that one suffix

80
00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.580
separate the entire set at

81
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.980
once. But distinguishing just adjacent

82
00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.940
members of an infinite sequence

83
00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.400
is insufficient unless you also

84
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.220
cover every other distinct pair.

85
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.060
Keep those quantifiers explicit in

86
00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:44.440
your proof.

87
00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.320
Things to take note of.

88
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.580
State the prefix set, take

89
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.580
an arbitrary distinct pair, and

90
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.080
give one common suffix with

91
00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.420
opposite membership outcomes. Use an

92
00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.700
infinite set for nonregularity, or

93
00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.360
combine a finite lower bound

94
00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.840
with a matching construction for

95
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.500
minimality; the excluded appendix proof

96
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.740
does not remove the need

97
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:04.700
to apply the theorem.
